{
  "id": "v02",
  "title": "Antonyms & Contrast Clues",
  "intro": "An antonym is a word that expresses a meaning directly contrary to that of another word. In the WBPSC Clerkship examination, questions rarely test basic school-level opposites (like good/bad); they intensely test words with negative prefixes (un-, in-, dis-, mis-, non-, a-, anti-), nuanced formal vocabulary, and contextual opposites where a word can have multiple distinct antonyms depending on sentence context.",
  "bengali": "Antonym বা বিপরীতার্থক শব্দ হলো এমন শব্দ যা অন্য একটি শব্দের সম্পূর্ণ বিপরীত অর্থ প্রকাশ করে।\n\nWBPSC Clerkship পরীক্ষায় Antonym সমাধানের ৪টি প্রধান বৈজ্ঞানিক কৌশল:\n১. Prefix Analysis (উপসর্গ বিশ্লেষণ):\n   অনেক শব্দের পূর্বে নির্দিষ্ট Prefix যুক্ত করে বিপরীত শব্দ গঠিত হয়:\n   - in- (inactive, incomplete, infallible, innocuous)\n   - un- (unfriendly, unstable, unyielding)\n   - dis- (dishonest, disrespect, disappear, disparity)\n   - mis- (mislead, misunderstand, misconduct)\n   - il- / im- / ir- (illogical, immortal, impossible, irresponsible)\n২. Tone and Connotation (শব্দের সুর ও ইতিবাচক/নেতিবাচক মাত্রা):\n   প্রশ্নে দেওয়া শব্দটি যদি ইতিবাচক (Positive) অর্থ প্রকাশ করে (যেমন: Benevolent, Amiable, Lucrative), তবে তার Antonym নিশ্চিতভাবে কোনো নেতিবাচক (Negative) শব্দ হবে। অপশনগুলোর অর্থ পুরোপুরি না জানলেও শুধুমাত্র Tone ম্যাচিং করে ২টি অপশন সঙ্গে সঙ্গে বাদ দেওয়া যায়।\n৩. Contextual Antonyms (প্রেক্ষাপটভিত্তিক বিপরীত শব্দ):\n   একই শব্দের একাধিক বিপরীতার্থক শব্দ থাকতে পারে। যেমন: 'Light'-এর বিপরীত 'Dark' (আলোর ক্ষেত্রে), কিন্তু ওজনের ক্ষেত্রে 'Heavy'; 'Fresh'-এর বিপরীত খাবারের ক্ষেত্রে 'Stale', কিন্তু অভিজ্ঞতার ক্ষেত্রে 'Experienced'।\n৪. Root Word Mastery (মূল শব্দাংশ):\n   Bene- (ভালো) বনাম Male- (খারাপ): যেমন Benevolent (দয়ালু) <-> Malevolent (বিদ্বেষী); Benefactor (উপকারী) <-> Malefactor (অপরাধী)।",
  "rules": [
    {
      "rule": "PREFIX REVERSAL RULES: English creates precise antonyms by prefixing Latin and Greek negative particles.",
      "examples": [
        "In- / Im- / Il- / Ir-: Capable -> Incapable; Patient -> Impatient; Legible -> Illegible; Rational -> Irrational.",
        "Dis-: Harmony -> Disharmony; Approve -> Disapprove; Honest -> Dishonest.",
        "Un-: Yielding -> Unyielding; Precedented -> Unprecedented; Scrupulous -> Unscrupulous.",
        "A- (without): Moral -> Amoral; Typical -> Atypical; Symmetric -> Asymmetric."
      ],
      "exceptions": [
        "Beware of false prefixes: 'Valuable' and 'Invaluable' are SYNONYMS (both mean priceless); 'Flammable' and 'Inflammable' both mean easily set on fire!"
      ]
    },
    {
      "rule": "ROOT WORD CONTRASTS (Bene- vs Mal-; Eu- vs Dys-; Phil- vs Mis-):",
      "examples": [
        "Benevolent (kind, generous) <-> Malevolent (spiteful, malicious).",
        "Benefactor (one who supports) <-> Malefactor (criminal, wrongdoer).",
        "Euphoria (intense joy) <-> Dysphoria (deep unease or dissatisfaction).",
        "Philanthropist (lover of humanity) <-> Misanthrope (hater of humanity)."
      ],
      "exceptions": [
        "Root words provide instantaneous elimination shortcuts when evaluating unfamiliar options in the exam hall."
      ]
    },
    {
      "rule": "CONTEXTUAL ANTONYMS: Many common English words have divergent antonyms depending on their semantic domain.",
      "examples": [
        "BARREN (land) <-> Fertile; BARREN (discussion) <-> Fruitful / Productive.",
        "ACUTE (angle) <-> Obtuse; ACUTE (pain / mind) <-> Mild / Dull.",
        "OBSOLETE (technology / law) <-> Modern / Contemporary / In vogue.",
        "CANDID (frank, truthful) <-> Secretive / Evasive / Deceitful."
      ],
      "exceptions": [
        "Always read the word in context or identify the primary dictionary register intended by the exam question."
      ]
    },
    {
      "rule": "DEGREE AND INTENSITY POLARITY: An extreme adjective requires an equally extreme or appropriately scaled opposite.",
      "examples": [
        "Colossal (gigantic, immense) <-> Tiny / Miniature / Minute.",
        "Zenith (highest peak, acme) <-> Nadir (lowest depth, rock-bottom).",
        "Opulent (lavishly wealthy) <-> Destitute / Impoverished / Indigent.",
        "Meticulous (extremely thorough) <-> Careless / Sloppy / Negligent."
      ],
      "exceptions": [
        "Do not match a highly formal literary word with a casual colloquial opposite if a formal antonym exists among the options."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "Thinking 'Invaluable' is the opposite of 'Valuable' (they are synonyms; the opposite is 'worthless').",
    "Thinking 'Inflammable' means non-flammable (it means easily ignited; opposite is 'non-flammable').",
    "Choosing an antonym with a mismatched part of speech (an adjective must be paired with an adjective).",
    "Confusing 'Nadir' (lowest point, opposite of zenith) with 'Apex' (highest point, synonym of zenith)."
  ],
  "examTricks": [
    "The Tone Elimination Shortcut: Label the question word as (+) or (-). The correct antonym MUST have the opposite polarity (+ becomes -, - becomes +).",
    "The 'Invaluable' Trap: Remember: Valuable = মূল্যবান; Invaluable = অমূল্য (synonym, not antonym). Worthless = মূল্যহীন (true antonym).",
    "The Zenith-Nadir Pair: Zenith = শীর্ষবিন্দু (highest); Nadir = পাদদেশ (lowest). Recurring WBPSC favorite."
  ],
  "memoryTricks": [
